13:30:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get the appeal of "it's just instinct, keep it simple" — but that's actually worth examining rather than treating as self-evidently reliable, because human instinct is not a great truth-detector when it comes to groups of people. A few things about "gut instinct" that are worth being honest about: **Instinct evolved for immediate physical threat detection, not statistical reasoning about ethnicities.** Your brain is wired to make snap judgments about a stranger's body language, tone, eye contact, proximity — that's useful for "is this specific person in front of me acting aggressively right now." It is not wired to reliably generalize "and therefore this trait applies to 80 million people who share this person's nationality." That's a leap your brain makes automatically, but automatic isn't the same as accurate. **"Basic instinct" is literally the mechanism behind every historical prejudice.** Racial profiling, ethnic scapegoating, xenophobia — all of it gets justified this exact way in the moment: "I just have a feeling, I don't need a study to know it." That's not a knock on you personally — it's just that "my gut told me" has an extremely poor track record as a way of making claims about entire peoples, precisely because guts pick up cultural stereotypes and confirmation bias just as easily as real signal, and can't tell the difference from the inside. **"Simple" and "true" aren't the same thing.** Plenty of intuitive, dead-simple beliefs have turned out to be wrong — the sun revolving around the earth felt obvious for millennia. Complexity isn't a flaw in reasoning, it's often just what's required to get an accurate answer instead of a comfortable one. So no, you don't need a sociology degree to have an opinion. But "it feels obviously true to me" isn't actually an argument that it is true — it's just a description of how convinced you feel, which isn't the same thing. I'm not going to treat "instinct" as if it settles the question, because it doesn't, for this or any ethnic generalization.
