13:33:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely good catch, and I want to correct the overstatement rather than let it stand, because you're right that "nationality/culture means literally nothing" would be an extreme and false claim. Culture absolutely shapes people — real, measurable things: language, communication style, values around family/hierarchy/individualism, food, holidays, norms around directness or hospitality, religious practice, attitudes toward authority, etc. Cross-cultural psychology is a legitimate field precisely because culture does produce statistical tendencies in some domains — things like collectivism vs. individualism, comfort with ambiguity, directness in negotiation. I wouldn't defend the claim that culture is meaningless, because it isn't, and I don't think I actually said that — I've been arguing against something narrower. The specific claim I've been pushing back on isn't "culture shapes nothing," it's "culture reliably produces or fails to produce moral character traits like honesty/dishonesty, trustworthiness/untrustworthiness, at the level of an entire nationality." That's a different, much stronger claim, and it's the one that doesn't hold up. Here's the distinction: - "Iranian culture places high value on hospitality and poetic language" — culture-level claim, reasonably supportable. - "Iranian culture tends to have more indirect communication norms than American culture" — culture-level claim, studied in cross-cultural communication research, reasonably supportable. - "Iranian culture makes people shady/untrustworthy" — this isn't really a "culture" claim at all, it's a moral character claim, and moral character (honesty, integrity, trustworthiness) doesn't cluster by nationality the way that, say, communication style or food preferences do. Honest and dishonest people exist within every culture in roughly comparable proportions, because those traits track more with individual psychology, family upbringing, personal incentives, and circumstance than with national identity. So: culture shapes plenty. It doesn't reliably shape "is this person trustworthy," which is the specific claim in question here. That's not an extreme "culture means nothing" position — it's a narrower point about which traits culture actually predicts and which it doesn't.
